    "title": "Video: Suspect tries to hide from police under blanket in vacant building, still gets caught - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Will Conybeare ,\n7 hours ago\nPolice in Upland arrested a domestic violence suspect who tried to evade capture by hiding under a blanket in a vacant building.\nThe Upland Police Department stated on social media that officers responded to the area of 11 th Street and Berry Court around 9 a.m. last Wednesday, Sept. 17, for reports of a woman who had been assaulted.\n“Officers located the victim, who was bleeding from her eye and foot, who said she was attacked by her ex-boyfriend,” UPD said.\nThe suspect had fled the area prior to officers arriving; however, they were able to identify him as a transient known to trespass in a vacant building in the area.\nCostly commode controversy: Residents heated over million-dollar bathroom in L.A. park About three hours later, according to the bodycam footage, officers searched the building as a drone surveilled the scene from overhead and eventually found the man laying under a blanket in the empty building, which was littered with garbage and covered in graffiti.\nPolice arrest a domestic violence suspect hiding under blanket in a vacant building in Upland. Sept. 2025. (X/@UplandPD) Police arrest a domestic violence suspect hiding under blanket in a vacant building in Upland. Sept. 2025. (X/@UplandPD) There was a bed, a nightstand, a clothing rack and a wardrobe near where the man was hiding, the bodycam footage showed, indicating he may have been staying there for some time.\n“Come on man, why are you hiding?” one of the officers can be heard asking the man, who responded with “What do you mean ‘Why am I hiding?’ I’m trespassing, I’m not supposed to be here.”\nThe man, who was not named, was arrested for felony domestic violence.\nIrvine police seek ‘Postal Malone’ in package theft “[He] won’t be putting his hands on any women where he’s going,” Upland police said in their social media post.\nUPD advised anyone in a volatile relationship or anyone who knows someone in a volatile relationship to report it to law enforcement right away.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to KTLA.\n5",
